WebKit: the network cache's file times have a body on a port with neither st_birthtime nor GLib.

NetworkCache::fileTimes() is three #if branches (HAVE(STAT_BIRTHTIME), USE(SOUP), OS(WINDOWS)) and
nothing else: on a port that is none of them the function has no body, falls off its end (undefined
behaviour) and clang compiles every path that reaches it to a trap. updateFileModificationTime()'s
lambda is then `push rbp; mov rbp,rsp; int3`, so the first cache *hit* of a second boot (the
record is read, its modification time is refreshed on the serial background queue) killed a
NetworkProcess thread, and the cache's shrink and "clear since" would have done the same.

LazyOS has no st_birthtime (musl) and no GLib to emulate it with an xattr, so the creation time is
not known; the fallback reports the modification time (which stat gives) as both.

--- a/Source/WebKit/NetworkProcess/cache/NetworkCacheFileSystem.cpp
+++ b/Source/WebKit/NetworkProcess/cache/NetworkCacheFileSystem.cpp
@@ -82,6 +82,16 @@
     auto createTime = FileSystem::fileCreationTime(path);
     auto modifyTime = FileSystem::fileModificationTime(path);
     return { valueOrDefault(createTime), valueOrDefault(modifyTime) };
+#else
+    // No st_birthtime (musl) and no xattr birthtime (that is written by the GLib IO channel): the
+    // creation time of a record is not known. Report its modification time as both, so a record's
+    // worth is 0 ("nothing known": the shrink treats all records alike) and
+    // updateFileModificationTimeIfNeeded refreshes it on every hit, which keeps the modification
+    // time (the only one that is real) a last-used time.
+    auto modifyTime = FileSystem::fileModificationTime(path);
+    if (!modifyTime)
+        return { };
+    return { *modifyTime, *modifyTime };
 #endif
 }
 
